WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:02.060
- [Narrator] Gordon Ramsey

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.600
is heading to Kwazulu-Natal, South Africa

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.330
to learn the secrets of Zulu cuisine.

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:11.150
But how the region developed
some of its signature dishes,

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.423
reveals a deeper and darker history.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.690
In just one stroll through a spice market,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.290
in Durban, South Africa,

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:24.420
you can find the perfect
marriage of Indo-African flavor.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.340
From the touch of spice
and a fresh Ushatini

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.670
to the powerful heat found
in traditional Durban Curry.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.980
But how did Indian spices find their way

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.010
into South African cuisine?

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.570
- The Zulu palette is very simplistic,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.310
but we do add a touch of
chili every now and then.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:42.685
- Right.

00:00:42.685 --> 00:00:43.875
- Because that sort of culture

00:00:43.875 --> 00:00:46.030
has infiltrated throughout the region.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.430
- [Narrator] As of 2019,

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.630
there are approximately 1.5 million people

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.693
of Indian descent currently
living in South Africa.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.300
In 1860, the British
began bringing in Indians

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.423
to what is now known as
the Kwazulu-Natal Province.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.070
This wave of people were
predominantly indentured servants

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:09.400
who came to work in South
Africa sugarcane industry.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.520
India abandoned indentured
labor immigration

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.500
to the province in 1911.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.810
But with the end of indentured servitude,

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.860
came the beginning of apartheid in 1948,

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.810
the sanction racial
segregation and political

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.680
and economic discrimination
against non-whites.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.430
Prior to and during the apartheid era,

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:31.270
South African Indian
cuisine came from Indians,

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.150
reworking their own classic dishes

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.200
with the ingredients they had available

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.160
and combining the elements and new ways.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.020
Take one of Durban signature
dishes, Durban Curry.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.080
When the first groups
of indentured servants

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.050
came to the Kwazulu-Natal Province,

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:50.320
ingredients that they were long
accustomed to, were scarce.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.650
To make meals stretch,
local varieties of potatoes

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.100
were added into the curry,

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.930
which acted as a base to
the abundant amount of meat

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.470
and poultry available.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:03.490
Durban Curry also garnered its
iconic red hot flavor profile

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.700
due to its incorporation
of Indian hot chilies.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.580
- Oh my God, that's hot.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.480
- I thought you looked
like a spicy guy to me.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.930
- [Narrator] Another
example, Durban's Bunny Chow.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:17.130
The meal is made to be
eaten with your hands

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.300
and it was a more durable
spin on Indian bean Curry

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.530
with roti.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.430
It consists of a loaf of white bread

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.100
with the center hollowed out

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.280
and stuffed with curry.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.530
Several origins tie the dish
to the Indian working class

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.500
and the prejudice conditions
of the apartheid era.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.960
A popular origin suggests
that it was used as a way

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.360
to serve people of color
who were forbidden to eat

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.610
in cafes and restaurants.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.300
Nonwhite customers had
to order their food to go

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.600
at the door and the dense
bread served as a robust

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.883
and portable container.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.820
From indentured servitude to apartheid,

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.510
Indian cuisine in South Africa

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.890
was not just used as a way
to fill hungry bellies.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.720
It was used as a way to preserve a culture

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.460
and helped create a
new one in the process.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.043
(upbeat music)

